TALK = []
def tk(id, level, voice, title, text, qs): TALK.append({"id": id, "level": level, "voice": voice, "title": title, "text": text, "questions": qs})
tk("tk01",1,"W","Why Leaves Change Color","Today I want to explain why leaves change color in the fall. Leaves are green during spring and summer because they contain a lot of chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is the chemical that plants use to turn sunlight into food. But leaves also contain other colors, like yellow and orange. We just can't see them in summer because there is so much green. In the fall, the days get shorter and colder. Trees stop making chlorophyll, and the green color slowly disappears. That's when the yellow and orange colors become visible. Some trees, like maples, also make a new red color in the fall. Scientists think this red color may protect the leaves from too much sunlight while the tree takes back nutrients before the leaves drop.",[
 mc("What is the talk mainly about?","Why leaves change color in the fall",["How trees grow new leaves","Why some trees lose their leaves in summer","How plants are harmed by sunlight"],"Das Thema wird im ersten Satz genannt."),
 mc("Why are yellow and orange colors hard to see in summer?","The green chlorophyll covers them.",["They only form in winter.","Sunlight destroys them.","They are stored inside the branches."],"„we can't see them in summer because there is so much green“."),
 mc("What happens to chlorophyll in the fall?","Trees stop producing it.",["It turns red.","Trees produce more of it.","It moves to the roots."],"„Trees stop making chlorophyll“."),
 mc("According to the speaker, why might maple leaves turn red?","To protect the leaves while the tree takes back nutrients",["To attract insects","To keep the tree warm","To produce chlorophyll faster"],"Rot schützt vermutlich vor zu viel Sonne, während Nährstoffe zurückgeholt werden.")])
tk("tk02",1,"M","The First Bicycles","Let's look at the history of the bicycle. The first bicycle-like machine was built in Germany in 1817. It was made of wood and had no pedals. Riders sat on it and pushed themselves along with their feet. It was called a running machine. About fifty years later, pedals were added to the front wheel. These bicycles were faster, but they were also very uncomfortable. People called them boneshakers, because the roads were rough and the wheels were made of iron. Later, some bicycles had a huge front wheel and a tiny back wheel. A bigger wheel meant more speed, but riders often fell. Finally, in the 1880s, the safety bicycle appeared. It had two wheels of the same size and a chain connected to the back wheel, very similar to the bikes we ride today.",[
 mc("What was special about the first bicycle-like machine?","It had no pedals.",["It had a chain.","It was made of iron.","It had three wheels."],"„It was made of wood and had no pedals.“"),
 mc("Why were some early bicycles called boneshakers?","They were very uncomfortable to ride.",["They were extremely fast.","They were often stolen.","They were made of bones."],"Holprige Straßen und Eisenräder."),
 mc("What was the problem with bicycles that had a huge front wheel?","Riders often fell off.",["They were too slow.","They were too expensive.","They had no seats."],"„riders often fell“."),
 mc("What does the speaker say about the safety bicycle?","It was similar to modern bicycles.",["It was invented in 1817.","It had no chain.","It was only used in Germany."],"„very similar to the bikes we ride today“.")])
tk("tk03",2,"W","Desire Paths","Have you ever noticed a narrow dirt path cutting across a lawn, even though a paved sidewalk goes around it? Urban planners call these desire paths. They form when enough people decide that the official route is inconvenient and take a shortcut instead. For a long time, many planners saw desire paths as a nuisance. They put up fences or signs to keep people on the sidewalks. More recently, though, some designers have started treating these paths as valuable information. After all, a desire path shows exactly where people actually want to walk. Some universities have even used this idea when designing new campuses. Instead of paving walkways right away, they plant grass everywhere and wait a few months. Then they pave the paths that students have worn into the ground. The result is a network of walkways that matches how people really move around the campus.",[
 mc("What is the talk mainly about?","Paths created by shortcuts and how planners respond to them",["The history of paved sidewalks","How to grow grass on university campuses","Why people should stay on sidewalks"],"Trampelpfade und der Umgang der Planer damit."),
 mc("How did many planners traditionally respond to desire paths?","They tried to stop people from using them.",["They paved them immediately.","They studied them carefully.","They ignored them completely."],"Zäune und Schilder – „a nuisance“."),
 mc("Why does the speaker mention universities?","To give an example of designers using desire paths in planning",["To show where desire paths are most common","To criticize students for damaging lawns","To explain who first described desire paths"],"Beispiel: erst Gras säen, dann die Trampelpfade pflastern."),
 mc("What can be inferred about the walkways at those universities?","They are likely to be convenient for students.",["They were designed before the campus was built.","They are mostly made of dirt.","They are usually fenced in."],"Sie folgen den Wegen, die Studierende tatsächlich gehen.")])
tk("tk04",2,"M","The Tragedy of the Commons","In economics, there's an idea called the tragedy of the commons. Imagine a pasture that's open to all the farmers in a village. Each farmer benefits from adding one more cow to the field, because they get the milk and the profit. But the cost, the grass that the extra cow eats, is shared by everyone. So each farmer has a reason to keep adding cows. Eventually, the pasture is overgrazed and becomes useless for everyone. The same pattern shows up in many modern situations: overfishing in the ocean, traffic on public roads, even air pollution. In each case, individuals acting in their own interest end up harming a resource they all depend on. For many years, economists thought the only solutions were government rules or private ownership. But the political scientist Elinor Ostrom found that many communities manage shared resources successfully on their own, by creating local rules and monitoring each other. Her work earned her a Nobel Prize in 2009.",[
 mc("What is the main purpose of the talk?","To explain a concept and how the problem can be addressed",["To describe the history of farming","To argue that all resources should be private","To compare cows with fish"],"Begriff erklären + Lösungen (Regeln, Ostrom)."),
 mc("Why does each farmer keep adding cows?","Each farmer gets the benefit, while the cost is shared.",["The government pays farmers for each cow.","The pasture keeps growing.","Cows produce more milk in large groups."],"Nutzen privat, Kosten geteilt."),
 mc("Why does the speaker mention traffic on public roads?","As a modern example of the same problem",["To show a solution to overgrazing","To explain how roads are funded","To describe Ostrom's research"],"Aufzählung moderner Beispiele."),
 mc("What did Elinor Ostrom find?","Communities can manage shared resources with their own rules.",["Government rules are always necessary.","Private ownership is the only solution.","Shared resources always fail."],"Lokale Regeln und gegenseitige Kontrolle funktionieren.")])
tk("tk05",2,"W","How Birds Navigate","How do migrating birds find their way over thousands of kilometers? Researchers have discovered that birds use several tools at once. During the day, many species navigate using the position of the sun. At night, some birds, like the indigo bunting, use the stars. In one famous experiment, young buntings were raised in a planetarium, a building where the night sky is projected onto a ceiling. When the researchers rotated the projected sky, the birds changed the direction they tried to fly. Birds also seem to sense the Earth's magnetic field. Scientists believe that special proteins in birds' eyes may allow them to see magnetic lines. And as they get closer to their destination, birds rely on familiar landmarks, like rivers, coastlines, and mountains, as well as smells. What's remarkable is that birds can switch between these tools depending on the weather. On cloudy nights, when the stars are hidden, they depend more on the magnetic field.",[
 mc("What is the talk mainly about?","The different ways birds navigate during migration",["Why birds migrate in winter","How planetariums are built","Which birds fly the longest distances"],"Mehrere Orientierungshilfen der Zugvögel."),
 mc("What did the planetarium experiment show?","The birds used the stars to choose a direction.",["The birds could not fly indoors.","The birds preferred daylight.","The birds ignored the projected sky."],"Gedrehter Himmel → andere Flugrichtung."),
 mc("According to the speaker, what may help birds sense the magnetic field?","Proteins in their eyes",["Their feathers","Landmarks such as rivers","The position of the sun"],"„special proteins in birds' eyes“."),
 mc("What do birds do on cloudy nights?","They rely more on the magnetic field.",["They stop flying until morning.","They follow other birds.","They use the sun instead."],"Sterne verdeckt → Magnetfeld.")])
tk("tk06",3,"M","Anchoring","I'd like to talk about a cognitive bias that affects nearly every negotiation you'll ever take part in: anchoring. Anchoring refers to our tendency to rely too heavily on the first piece of information we receive when making a judgment. In a classic study, participants spun a wheel that was rigged to stop at either ten or sixty-five. They were then asked to estimate the percentage of African countries in the United Nations. Those who'd seen sixty-five gave estimates that were, on average, almost twice as high as those who'd seen ten, even though they knew the number was random. Now, the implications for business are considerable. In salary negotiations, the first figure mentioned tends to shape the entire discussion, which is why some experts advise making the first offer yourself, provided you've done your research. Interestingly, being aware of the bias doesn't eliminate it. Even experienced real estate agents, when given different listing prices for the same house, produced valuations that were pulled toward the listed price, while insisting the price hadn't influenced them.",[
 mc("What is the speaker mainly discussing?","The tendency for initial information to influence judgments",["Strategies for estimating percentages","The history of the United Nations","How real estate agents are trained"],"Anker-Effekt: erste Information prägt das Urteil."),
 mc("Why does the speaker mention the wheel experiment?","To show that even random numbers can affect estimates",["To explain how the United Nations was formed","To show that people are good at estimating","To compare two types of negotiation"],"Zufallszahl verzerrte die Schätzungen."),
 mc("What advice does the speaker mention for salary negotiations?","Consider making the first offer after doing research",["Always wait for the employer's offer","Never mention a specific number","Accept the first figure that is offered"],"„making the first offer yourself, provided you've done your research“."),
 mc("What does the speaker imply about the real estate agents?","They were influenced by the bias without realizing it.",["Their experience protected them from the bias.","They deliberately overvalued the houses.","They refused to give valuations."],"Beeinflusst, obwohl sie es bestritten.")])
tk("tk07",3,"W","Dating the Past with Tree Rings","Today we're going to look at dendrochronology, the science of dating events using tree rings. Most of you know that a tree adds one ring per year. What's less obvious is that the width of each ring reflects the growing conditions that year: wet years produce wide rings, while droughts produce narrow ones. Because trees in the same region experience the same weather, they share a distinctive pattern of wide and narrow rings, a bit like a barcode. Researchers can match the pattern in a living tree with the pattern in an old wooden beam, and then overlap that with even older samples. By chaining samples together in this way, scientists in Europe have built continuous records stretching back more than twelve thousand years. The applications go well beyond biology. Archaeologists have used tree rings to determine exactly when medieval buildings were constructed, and climate scientists use them to reconstruct rainfall patterns from periods long before weather records existed. Tree rings have even helped calibrate radiocarbon dating, correcting errors in that method of up to several centuries.",[
 mc("What is the main topic of the talk?","Using tree rings to date events and reconstruct the past",["How trees survive droughts","The history of medieval architecture","Problems with modern weather records"],"Dendrochronologie und ihre Anwendungen."),
 mc("Why does the speaker compare ring patterns to a barcode?","To show that the patterns are distinctive and can be matched",["To explain how machines count rings","To show that all trees have the same number of rings","To describe how wood is sold"],"Unverwechselbares Muster, das man abgleichen kann."),
 mc("How have scientists created records that go back more than 12,000 years?","By linking overlapping samples of different ages",["By finding a single very old tree","By using radiocarbon dating alone","By studying modern weather records"],"„By chaining samples together“."),
 mc("What does the speaker imply about radiocarbon dating?","It was less accurate before tree-ring data was used.",["It has replaced dendrochronology.","It can only be used on trees.","It is more accurate than tree rings."],"Baumringe korrigierten Fehler von bis zu mehreren Jahrhunderten.")])

